A||N||6||kornholio||torquing waterpump||||1
Z||000000||kornholio||10-12-1999||07:25 PM||||How important is it to torque the waterpump bolts correctly? I don't have a torque wrench. And how hard is it to replace on a V6? The Haynes instructions seem pretty easy.||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||10-12-1999||07:30 PM||||The torque value is not that improtant like say on the heads ot intake. The water pump r&r is pretty straight forward on the V6.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BENGAL4||10-12-1999||08:25 PM||||It's very easy to replace the water pump on the V6. One thing though, remove the fender well skirting so you have easy access to the bottom bolts. Also, get a piece of styrofoam and place the bolts in it as you remove them. This makes it easier to remember where they go. Finally, tighten from the middle out and criscross just like you put on a wheel. Good Luck.||||reg||14
Z||000003||kornholio||10-12-1999||09:59 PM||||So, it's not possible to remove all of the bolts from above? How many bolts do I have to remove while under the car?||||reg||5
Z||000004||Linx||10-12-1999||10:57 PM||||From under the car?  No way.  I changed mine last weekend- from the top.  I hadta move the EGR solenoid and bracket out of the way, but after that, it was all a matter of selecting the right extensions, (or none at all).<P>It was tougher to retension the belt.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Brian||10-13-1999||08:44 AM||||Yes, it is possible to change your waterpump (V6)from above. The real trick to it is to lossen the pulley bolts before you take of the belt! <BR>It's almost impossible to loosen the pulley bolts with out something to stop the pulley from spinning (that's why you still need the belt).<BR>There are 2 or 3 bolts on the bottom of the pump that are obstructed by the pulley. ||||reg||4
Z||000006||BENGAL4||10-13-1999||08:54 AM||||If you remove the skirting like I said, you do not have to get under the car. It makes it a 1 hour job instead of a 2 hour one. The bolts are right there in front of your face. If you can remove and replace the pump from the top, you must have skinny arms and small hands. Being 6' 1" and 260 lbs, any access area is small to me. One other tip, were a long sleeve shirt. That fiberglass insulation will eat you up.||||reg||12
